A||N||10||Verticalspeed||**Shaking @65-76**||||1||
Z||000000||Verticalspeed||02-12-2001||10:32 AM||||I installed BRAND New front tie rod ends. Did the 12-6 ball joint test. But at 65-75 my front end still shakes as if the wheel (left or right) is wobbling! -  Checked the bearings and spindle nuts. What  causes this scary symptom? Anyone care to share if they had the same problem and how you fixed it?<BR>Alignment is done and the car tracks straight. Tires have plenty of tread and the guys at Discount Tires said they look like they are not damaged.<BR>Rims seem to be o.k. Very slight steering wheel shimmy, almost insignificant.<P>Thank you!!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroJoe||02-12-2001||10:37 AM||||I have never had this problem, but could it possibly be something with the steering box, or the steering stabilizer?  Something mighta gotten shaken loose or something, just a thought.<P>------------------<BR>FieroJoe<BR>c311t@yahoo.com<BR>AIM: Fiero87311, Snowboarder0311<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||mbramble||02-12-2001||10:44 AM||||I had the same syptoms at just about the same speeds, but a 4 wheel alignment corrected the problem.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Verticalspeed||02-12-2001||10:48 AM||||The car is an 86 SE. I have also replaced the rear ball joints. The shaking seems to be coming from the front. I am buying a new set of front tires to see if the problem goes away. Front shocks are new. My shaking is a "left to right" or right to left (in front) not up and down.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Will||02-12-2001||10:56 AM||||You've done the 6-12 ball joint check, but you might also want to do the 3-9 steering hardware check (with the key off and the steering wheel lock engaged). Did you do inner tie rod ends or just outer? Is your rack preload set properly? When was the last time you had your wheels balanced?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Verticalspeed||02-12-2001||11:06 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B> do the 3-9 steering hardware check  d?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>O.K. with the front end off the ground, the steering wheel locked and doing the 9-3 test, the wheel WILL move 1/8 of an inch<BR>in and out. I have only replaced the outer tie rod ends. I figured with no load on the wheels, it would be normal to have that bit of slack. AM i wrong  assuming this?<BR>Maybe I need a new bushing or new inner tie rods? Tires were balanced twice. 2 weeks apart. Alingment is recent. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||87GT||02-12-2001||05:51 PM||||I had the same problem with my GT.  I had an alignment, new tie rod ends, ball joints were in good shape and I had tires that were only a year old.  I just recently decided to change the tires and it made a huge difference.  I still feel a little shake in the wheel but not like it was.  I have poly bushings that are going on next.  Hopefully, that will knock it out.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Will||02-12-2001||10:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Verticalspeed:<BR><B> O.K. with the front end off the ground, the steering wheel locked and doing the 9-3 test, the wheel WILL move 1/8 of an inch<BR>in and out. I have only replaced the outer tie rod ends. I figured with no load on the wheels, it would be normal to have that bit of slack. AM i wrong  assuming this?<BR>Maybe I need a new bushing or new inner tie rods? Tires were balanced twice. 2 weeks apart. Alingment is recent. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You shouldn't feel any play. Have someone lie under the car while you do this and try to see where the play is.<BR>Could be inner tie rod ends (grab the boot where the inner tie rod end should be and shake in various directions), the wheel bearings (helper will see movement between teh wheel and the brake rotor or spindle), rack preload (grab both inner tie rod ends and shake back and forth in unison), or worn out rack mounts.||||reg||1
Z||000008||ThatRickGuy||02-13-2001||11:13 AM||||Most likely the problem is tire balance. I've seen balance do anything from a slight hum, to minor vibrations, to making a car undriveable. If the alignment is good, and you have very little play, a very small ammount of play is exceptable, 1/16-1/8 inch sounds fine to me on the 3-9 test. Getting the tires balanced is cheap and fast anyways.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||stsmithgt||02-13-2001||04:07 PM||||I had that problem too.  Right after I put on a new set of low profile directional tires, Signet 205/60/15 all the way around.  I thought threw a weight off the tire, my alignment was out or I had a bent rim.  Nope.  I changed to a nice set of Michellin tires, 215/60/15 and the problem was gone.  I'm not saying it's a glassy smooth ride now, but it never was.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||ToddAsh||02-13-2001||07:48 PM||||I just put 16" wheels and tires on my Fiero {junk yard $150. LUCKY FIND) off of a Z-34 or Monte Carlo.Had a slight shake at 65MPH.Got tires balanced and now it's great. I'd balance before anything else. Todd||||reg||1
